1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,600
Two biotechnologies.

2
00:00:02,080 --> 00:00:04,400
A cotton plant that makes its own protein

3
00:00:04,560 --> 00:00:05,600
against caterpillars.

4
00:00:06,080 --> 00:00:09,120
And a dairy bull whose DNA was edited

5
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:11,760
so his calves would be born without horns.

6
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:13,400
Both work.

7
00:00:13,680 --> 00:00:15,720
But working isn't the same as being a

8
00:00:15,760 --> 00:00:17,040
good idea.

9
00:00:17,160 --> 00:00:19,360
So how should we judge plant and animal

10
00:00:19,440 --> 00:00:22,640
biotechnology, and why does an animal case need

11
00:00:22,840 --> 00:00:23,760
something extra?

12
00:00:24,967 --> 00:00:28,566
A judgement needs three things, each one concrete.

13
00:00:28,967 --> 00:00:32,166
A benefit: what improves, and by how much.

14
00:00:32,807 --> 00:00:35,446
A harm or risk: what could go wrong.

15
00:00:35,927 --> 00:00:39,366
And for each, who: the people, animals or

16
00:00:39,447 --> 00:00:42,326
ecosystems affected, called stakeholders.

17
00:00:43,047 --> 00:00:45,526
One technology can help one group and cost

18
00:00:45,727 --> 00:00:47,846
another, so a list of pros and cons

19
00:00:48,007 --> 00:00:50,726
isn't enough: say who gets which.

20
00:00:51,934 --> 00:00:53,453
Start with the plant.

21
00:00:53,774 --> 00:00:56,093
Bt cotton carries a gene from a soil

22
00:00:56,174 --> 00:00:57,093
bacterium.

23
00:00:57,374 --> 00:00:59,853
Its cells make a protein that's toxic to

24
00:00:59,894 --> 00:01:02,053
certain caterpillars that eat the plant.

25
00:01:02,334 --> 00:01:04,733
It only works after binding to sites in

26
00:01:04,854 --> 00:01:07,853
those insects' guts, which people and most other

27
00:01:08,054 --> 00:01:09,053
animals lack.

28
00:01:09,374 --> 00:01:12,653
Australian growers once sprayed insecticide many times a

29
00:01:12,734 --> 00:01:13,693
season.

30
00:01:13,894 --> 00:01:16,813
Together with better pest management, Bt cotton has

31
00:01:16,934 --> 00:01:19,613
cut that by about eighty-five per cent.

32
00:01:19,694 --> 00:01:23,693
That benefits growers, farm workers, nearby communities, and

33
00:01:23,774 --> 00:01:26,333
the beneficial insects that broad sprays used to

34
00:01:26,494 --> 00:01:26,813
kill.

35
00:01:28,034 --> 00:01:29,353
Now the risks.

36
00:01:29,634 --> 00:01:32,513
Caterpillars that happen to carry alleles for resistance

37
00:01:32,674 --> 00:01:35,953
survive, and pass them on: natural selection.

38
00:01:36,434 --> 00:01:40,353
Over generations, resistance could spread until Bt stops

39
00:01:40,434 --> 00:01:41,153
working.

40
00:01:41,394 --> 00:01:45,233
So Australian growers must also plant refuges, areas

41
00:01:45,314 --> 00:01:49,153
of non-Bt crops where susceptible caterpillars survive and

42
00:01:49,234 --> 00:01:52,593
mate with any resistant ones, keeping resistance alleles

43
00:01:52,674 --> 00:01:53,313
rare.

44
00:01:53,554 --> 00:01:56,113
Growers also pay a licence fee each season

45
00:01:56,154 --> 00:01:57,873
to the company that owns the trait.

46
00:01:58,354 --> 00:02:01,233
And Bt doesn't fix one thing: a field

47
00:02:01,314 --> 00:02:04,353
of one crop still holds little biodiversity.

48
00:02:05,567 --> 00:02:07,286
So, a judgement.

49
00:02:07,567 --> 00:02:10,526
For Australian cotton, the benefit is large and

50
00:02:10,687 --> 00:02:13,286
measured, and it reaches several groups.

51
00:02:13,567 --> 00:02:17,086
The main risk, resistance, is real but managed

52
00:02:17,167 --> 00:02:18,366
by refuges.

53
00:02:18,687 --> 00:02:21,326
Growers pay the fee, yet they've chosen Bt

54
00:02:21,407 --> 00:02:22,846
for most Australian cotton.

55
00:02:23,487 --> 00:02:26,846
On that evidence, Bt cotton is justified, as

56
00:02:26,927 --> 00:02:29,606
long as resistance management keeps working.

57
00:02:30,047 --> 00:02:32,206
Notice that a strong verdict comes with a

58
00:02:32,287 --> 00:02:33,006
condition.

59
00:02:34,234 --> 00:02:35,113
Now the animal.

60
00:02:35,194 --> 00:02:38,713
Most dairy cattle grow horns, and horns can

61
00:02:38,794 --> 00:02:41,033
injure other cattle and the people who handle

62
00:02:41,114 --> 00:02:41,353
them.

63
00:02:41,834 --> 00:02:44,873
So calves usually have their horn buds removed,

64
00:02:44,954 --> 00:02:46,233
which is painful.

65
00:02:46,634 --> 00:02:49,993
Some beef breeds, like Angus, are naturally hornless,

66
00:02:50,114 --> 00:02:51,753
because of one dominant allele.

67
00:02:52,394 --> 00:02:55,193
Crossing dairy cattle with Angus would also bring

68
00:02:55,274 --> 00:02:57,433
in beef alleles that lower milk yield.

69
00:02:57,994 --> 00:03:01,193
So scientists edited just that hornless allele into

70
00:03:01,274 --> 00:03:04,473
dairy cattle cells, and produced two bulls, born

71
00:03:04,514 --> 00:03:07,273
in 2015, whose calves could inherit it.

72
00:03:07,914 --> 00:03:11,033
Here the benefit goes to the animals themselves:

73
00:03:11,114 --> 00:03:12,073
no dehorning.

74
00:03:13,300 --> 00:03:17,140
But in 2019, government scientists checking one bull's

75
00:03:17,220 --> 00:03:20,460
DNA found something no one had intended.

76
00:03:20,740 --> 00:03:24,580
Bacterial DNA used in the editing, including antibiotic

77
00:03:24,660 --> 00:03:28,340
resistance genes, had been inserted beside the edit,

78
00:03:28,460 --> 00:03:30,820
and four of his six tested calves had

79
00:03:30,900 --> 00:03:31,940
inherited it.

80
00:03:32,260 --> 00:03:34,740
The calves were healthy, but the company hadn't

81
00:03:34,820 --> 00:03:35,460
checked for it.

82
00:03:36,020 --> 00:03:38,580
So the idea can still be justified, but

83
00:03:38,740 --> 00:03:40,820
only for animals checked to carry the edit

84
00:03:40,900 --> 00:03:41,780
alone.

85
00:03:42,100 --> 00:03:45,380
Judge what a biotechnology actually did, by checking

86
00:03:45,460 --> 00:03:46,260
the evidence.

87
00:03:47,467 --> 00:03:49,626
That's what makes animal cases different.

88
00:03:50,027 --> 00:03:52,746
An animal can suffer, so its welfare is

89
00:03:52,787 --> 00:03:54,426
a criterion of its own.

90
00:03:55,067 --> 00:03:58,826
Producing edited animals also involves embryos and surrogate

91
00:03:58,907 --> 00:04:01,786
cows, and many embryos don't survive.

92
00:04:02,347 --> 00:04:04,266
And some people object to editing animals at

93
00:04:04,347 --> 00:04:06,026
all, whatever the outcome.

94
00:04:07,234 --> 00:04:10,393
Those objections sort into three kinds of question.

95
00:04:10,674 --> 00:04:15,073
Consequences: what actually happens, to health, yield or

96
00:04:15,114 --> 00:04:15,873
the environment.

97
00:04:16,354 --> 00:04:17,793
Evidence can answer these.

98
00:04:18,434 --> 00:04:21,753
Distribution: who gains and who pays, like the

99
00:04:21,794 --> 00:04:22,593
licence fee.

100
00:04:23,074 --> 00:04:24,993
Rules and policy decide those.

101
00:04:25,554 --> 00:04:28,433
And principle: is it acceptable at all?

102
00:04:28,674 --> 00:04:30,353
Evidence can't settle that one.

103
00:04:31,567 --> 00:04:32,966
So here's a checklist.

104
00:04:33,247 --> 00:04:34,366
Who benefits?

105
00:04:34,607 --> 00:04:35,926
Who carries the risk?

106
00:04:36,207 --> 00:04:38,166
Who decides whether it's used?

107
00:04:38,367 --> 00:04:40,806
And what happens to biodiversity?

108
00:04:41,087 --> 00:04:43,806
For an animal, add one more: what happens

109
00:04:43,847 --> 00:04:45,166
to the animal itself.

110
00:04:45,647 --> 00:04:48,366
And when a question says evaluate, finish with

111
00:04:48,447 --> 00:04:50,526
a verdict and its main reason.

112
00:04:51,734 --> 00:04:53,173
Two exam questions now.

113
00:04:53,654 --> 00:04:54,373
First.

114
00:04:54,694 --> 00:04:57,333
Golden Rice has been genetically modified to make

115
00:04:57,414 --> 00:05:00,693
beta-carotene, which the body converts to vitamin A.

116
00:05:01,334 --> 00:05:04,213
It was developed to reduce vitamin A deficiency,

117
00:05:04,294 --> 00:05:07,173
which can cause blindness, in communities where rice

118
00:05:07,254 --> 00:05:08,213
is the main food.

119
00:05:08,934 --> 00:05:11,653
Identify one group that could benefit, and one

120
00:05:11,734 --> 00:05:12,853
possible concern.

121
00:05:13,494 --> 00:05:16,053
Then explain why evidence that it's safe to

122
00:05:16,214 --> 00:05:19,053
eat wouldn't settle every objection to it.

123
00:05:19,414 --> 00:05:20,693
Pause here to answer.

124
00:05:31,100 --> 00:05:34,780
Children in rice-dependent communities could benefit, because vitamin

125
00:05:34,860 --> 00:05:36,940
A deficiency hits them hardest.

126
00:05:37,580 --> 00:05:40,460
One concern: whether people will grow it and

127
00:05:40,540 --> 00:05:41,620
eat enough.

128
00:05:42,060 --> 00:05:45,260
Safety is a question of consequences, so evidence

129
00:05:45,340 --> 00:05:46,060
can answer it.

130
00:05:46,540 --> 00:05:48,940
But an objection of principle, or about who

131
00:05:49,020 --> 00:05:51,740
controls the seed, isn't about safety.

132
00:05:52,300 --> 00:05:54,780
Saying it's safe, so nothing's left to object

133
00:05:54,900 --> 00:05:56,860
to, is the classic mistake.

134
00:05:58,067 --> 00:05:58,546
Second.

135
00:05:58,787 --> 00:06:01,986
AquAdvantage salmon carry a growth hormone gene from

136
00:06:02,187 --> 00:06:05,106
another salmon species, so they reach market size

137
00:06:05,267 --> 00:06:07,906
faster than ordinary Atlantic salmon.

138
00:06:07,987 --> 00:06:10,466
They are produced as all-female fish that are

139
00:06:10,627 --> 00:06:13,666
effectively sterile, and were raised in secure tanks

140
00:06:13,747 --> 00:06:14,666
on land.

141
00:06:15,027 --> 00:06:18,146
Evaluate the use of this biotechnology, referring to

142
00:06:18,227 --> 00:06:21,266
a benefit, a risk and the groups affected.

143
00:06:21,587 --> 00:06:22,866
Pause here to answer.

144
00:06:33,267 --> 00:06:36,146
Fish farmers benefit, because their salmon reach market

145
00:06:36,227 --> 00:06:36,906
sooner.

146
00:06:37,187 --> 00:06:40,226
The main risk is escape, and modified salmon

147
00:06:40,307 --> 00:06:41,826
breeding with wild ones.

148
00:06:42,067 --> 00:06:44,786
Effective sterility and land tanks make that very

149
00:06:44,867 --> 00:06:47,666
unlikely, which protects wild salmon and the people

150
00:06:47,747 --> 00:06:48,786
who fish for them.

151
00:06:49,027 --> 00:06:51,386
The fish's welfare still needs checking.

152
00:06:51,587 --> 00:06:54,266
So it's justified under these controls.

153
00:06:54,547 --> 00:06:57,186
Listing pros and cons with no verdict is

154
00:06:57,227 --> 00:06:58,626
the classic mistake.

155
00:06:59,834 --> 00:07:03,353
So to judge any biotechnology: name a concrete

156
00:07:03,434 --> 00:07:06,473
benefit and who gets it, a concrete harm

157
00:07:06,554 --> 00:07:09,353
or risk and who carries it, and, for

158
00:07:09,394 --> 00:07:10,953
an animal, its welfare.

159
00:07:11,594 --> 00:07:14,153
Check what the evidence shows, say which kind

160
00:07:14,194 --> 00:07:16,473
of question you're answering, and end with a

161
00:07:16,554 --> 00:07:18,153
verdict and its condition.

162
00:07:18,794 --> 00:07:22,233
Next, where biotechnology might go in the future.

163
00:07:23,434 --> 00:07:24,873
Here are the key notes.

164
00:07:25,114 --> 00:07:27,033
Pause the video if you need more time

165
00:07:27,114 --> 00:07:27,913
to copy them.
